{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nThe original macOS CI incident was observed after pytest passed: owner-nonwritable `.rebar/run_snapshots` trees made recursive basetemp removal fail. Commit `7d908fbbc6` removed the rejected RAM-disk workflow step and added cleanup only to `tests/unit/workflow/test_snapshot.py:19-24`. Current caller inventory finds one other module, `test_prior_art_hardening.py::test_sweep_removes_readonly_snapshot_tree`, but it removes its snapshot in-body. The remaining defect is structural: cleanup is owned by one producer module rather than the shared pytest harness, so the next snapshot-producing test module recreates the basetemp failure. Production snapshots must remain read-only under `src/rebar/llm/workflow/snapshot.py:_chmod_readonly`.\n\n## Approach\nMove the exact `.rebar/run_snapshots` teardown rule from the one snapshot test module into `tests/conftest.py`. The new autouse fixture will depend directly on `tmp_path`; this adds no universal tmp-path materialization because existing autouse fixture `_isolate_user_config` at `tests/conftest.py:329` already does so. At teardown it will search only beneath that test-owned temp root for directories whose final components are `.rebar/run_snapshots`, then call the existing `rebar.llm.workflow.snapshot._rmtree_writable` seam. Remove the duplicate module-local fixture. This repairs the cleanup class at its ownership boundary without weakening read-only assertions, adding a new cleanup implementation, or applying a broad chmod to unrelated temp content.\n\n## Scope\n- `tests/conftest.py`: centrally remove exact run-snapshot cache roots below each test-owned `tmp_path`.\n- `tests/unit/workflow/test_snapshot.py`: remove the superseded module-local teardown fixture.\n- `tests/unit/test_ci_snapshot_fixture_cleanup.py`: add a snapshot-producing subject outside `test_snapshot.py` and prove nested basetemp removability.\n\n## Testing\n- RED oracle: a subject test in `test_ci_snapshot_fixture_cleanup.py` creates a real git repo under `tmp_path`, calls `snapshot_at_ref`, and asserts the published file is non-writable. A separate test runs that exact node in nested pytest with an explicit basetemp, proves nested pytest passed, then calls ordinary `shutil.rmtree(basetemp)`. Before shared cleanup, rmtree fails on the residual 0555/0444 tree.\n- GREEN parity: parameterize the same nested behavior oracle over `test_snapshot.py::test_snapshot_is_read_only` and the new cross-module subject. Both explicit basetemps must be ordinarily removable after nested pytest exits.\n- Negative boundary: both subject tests observe read-only production snapshots before teardown. The shared fixture targets only paths matching `.rebar/run_snapshots` below pytest-owned `tmp_path`; unrelated temp content is not chmodded or removed.\n- Run focused snapshot/cleanup and repository-isolation tests, then `make lint` and `make typecheck`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Nested pytest exits 0 for the snapshot-module subject; verified by `test_completed_snapshot_test_leaves_basetemp_removable[test_snapshot_is_read_only]`.\n- [ ] The snapshot-module nested basetemp is removable with ordinary `shutil.rmtree`; verified by the same test.\n- [ ] Nested pytest exits 0 for the cross-module subject; verified by `test_completed_snapshot_test_leaves_basetemp_removable[cross_module]`.\n- [ ] The cross-module nested basetemp is removable with ordinary `shutil.rmtree`; verified by the same test.\n- [ ] Production snapshots remain non-writable before teardown; verified by `test_snapshot_is_read_only` and `test_cross_module_snapshot_is_read_only`.\n- [ ] Cleanup removes only `.rebar/run_snapshots` roots below `tmp_path`; verified by a focused negative-boundary test retaining adjacent content.\n- [ ] The held-out oracle is RED before repair, GREEN afterward, and RED again when the old module-local-only cleanup is mutation-restored.\n- [ ] Focused tests pass.\n- [ ] `make lint` passes.\n- [ ] `make typecheck` passes."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787027844150120001,"uuid":"95ebaecc-bff8-43ec-b73c-4ba120d7f7f9"}